          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Ran space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ran
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ran X
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            The Ran space is defined, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topological
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebro-geometric
            contexts, for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#LurieTamagawaNumbers"}> Lurie 14, lectures 7-9
            ). It is naturally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stratified+space"} stratified space
            (
            %a{:href => "#AyalaFrancisTanaka14"}> Ayala-Francis-Tanaka 14, section 3.7
            )
          %p
            For cardinality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ran
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi i
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %mtext colim
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Fin
                    %mrow
                      %mi surj
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi i
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Strat
                  %mo →
                  %mi StTop
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ran_{\leq i} X = \text{colim} ( Fin_{surj,\leq i}^{op} \to Strat \to StTop )
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Fin
                    %mrow
                      %mi surj
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Fin_{surj,\leq i}
            has objects that are finite sets of cardinality at most
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is infinite the subscript is left off as above.
          %p
            It is named after
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ziv+Ran"} Ziv Ran
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The Ran space of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+contractible+space"} weakly contractible
            (
            %a{:href => "#BeilDrin04"}> BeilDrin04, p. 173
            ).
          %h2#constructions Constructions
          %h3#algebraic_case_ran_prestack Algebraic case: Ran Prestack
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi CAlg
                    %mi k
                  %mo →
                  %mi Spaces
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X: CAlg_k \to Spaces
            be a derived prestack. Consider the diagram in prestacks
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi fSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi PrSt
                    %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{(-)}: N(fSet) \to PrSt_k, I \mapsto X^I
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi fSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} fSet
            is the category of non-empty finite sets, with morphisms being surjective maps. The functor sends morphisms to the corresponding diagonal maps. The Ran prestack by definition is the (homotopy) colimit of this diagram:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ran
                    %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi colim
                    %mi I
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ran_X := colim_I X^I
          %h2#examples Examples
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ran
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Ran_{\leq 2k+1}(S^1) \;\simeq\; S^{2k+1}
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Bott52"}> Bott 52
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Tuffley02"} Tuffley 02
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} nonabelian Poincaré duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p Apparently the concept of the Ran space was first introduced in
          %ul
            %li#BeilDrin04
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Beilinson"}> Alexander Beilinson
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Drinfeld"} Vladimir Drinfeld
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Chiral algebras
              = succeed "," do
                %em 51
          %p Review includes
          %ul
            %li#AyalaFrancisTanaka14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Ayala"}> David Ayala
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Francis"} John Francis
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hiro+Lee+Tanaka"} Hiro Lee Tanaka
              = succeed "," do
                %em Local structures on stratified spaces
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.0501"} arXiv:1409.0501
          %p Textbook accounts include
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , section 5.5.1 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebra"} Higher Algebra
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ran_space"} Wikipedia
          %p
            Ran spaces of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            are discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Bott52
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raoul+Bott"}> Raoul Bott
                ,
                %em
                  On the third symmetric potency of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
            %li#Tuffley02
              %p
                Christopher Tuffley,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Finite subsets of
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0209077"} arXiv:math/0209077
          %p Rational functions as functions on the Ran space in the sense of functions on the complement of finitely many points as these range over all points, is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Gaitsgory11
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Gaitsgory"}> Dennis Gaitsgory
              ,
              %em Contractibility of the space of rational maps
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1108.1741"}> arXiv:1108.1741
              )
          %p
            Acyclicity of (some version of) the Ran space of a connected,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-projective+scheme"} quasi-projective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+closed+field"} algebraically closed field
            is shown in lecture 10 of
          %ul
            %li#LurieTamagawaNumbers
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Tamagawa Numbers via Nonabelian Poincare Duality (282y)
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/282y.html"} website
          %p
            Any flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasicoherent+sheaf"} quasicoherent sheaf
            on the Ran space of a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} algebraic variety
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            canonically acquires a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-module"} D-module
            structure as shown in
          %ul
            %li
              James Tao,
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-Excisive Functors, Canonical Connections, and Line Bundles on the Ran Space
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.07976"}> arXiv:1906.07976
              )
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            includes
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Deligne"}> Pierre Deligne
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Etingof"} Pavel Etingof
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"} Dan Freed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Kazhdan"} David Kazhdan
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Morgan"} John Morgan
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Morrison"} David Morrison
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              (eds.) volume II, part III of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantum+Fields+and+Strings"}> Quantum Fields and Strings
                  , A course for mathematicians
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.ias.edu/qft"} web version
          %h3#relation_to_configuration_spaces_of_points Relation to configuration spaces of points
          %p
            Expressing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rational cohomology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordered+configuration+spaces+of+points"} ordered configuration spaces of points
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+homology"} factorization homology
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ran+spaces"} Ran spaces
          %ul
            %li
              Quoc P. Ho,
              %em Higher representation stability for ordered configuration spaces and twisted commutative factorization algebras
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.00252"}> arXiv:2004.00252
              )
        .revisedby
